entry.content:
&lt;p&gt;I fall for accidental engineering. Every engineer does from time to time. It goes something like this: After an hour long unsuccessful debug session I don&#x27;t really know anymore how to continue. I start to use Google or my currently favorite LLM, which gives me (partially hallucinated) commands that &quot;fix the smoking gun&quot;. I run them. After all, I don&#x27;t really know what to do anymore, right? With some luck the problem disappears. Which of the actions taken did actually resolve the issue? And is it really resolved or did I just treat the symptoms? I don&#x27;t know.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the pre-LLM this happened occasionally. However, lately I overhear much more conversations that contain some form of &quot;Claude gave me some commands, I ran them and it fixed the problem.&quot;. &lt;strong&gt;But did it really?&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;LLMs are powerful tools. But with great power comes great responsibility. Don&#x27;t let your hard-earned engineering skills be watered down by the allure of a &quot;quick fix&quot; LLM solution. Do you really understand what solved the problem? If not, you are on the best track to become an &quot;accidental engineer&quot;.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You get assigned a Pull request, open it and see a method definition&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;cpp&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; extractPolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;();&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;};&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;and immediately wonder: Extract from what? Extract to where?&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Congratulations! You just found a piece of code that is not explicit about its intent. It probably does a lot, but you just don&#x27;t know what. It&#x27;s all hidden in the implementation.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While hiding implementation details is good, it is hard to strike the right balance between exposing details and hiding them. When reading code I like it to be explicit about its intent. Code that tells me what it does. Code that doesn&#x27;t leave me guessing. Code that is easy to read, understand and most importantly easy to reason about.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;implicit-code-increases-cognitive-load&quot;&gt;Implicit code increases &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;zakirullin&#x2F;cognitive-load&quot;&gt;cognitive load&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consider the following class:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;cpp&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;&#x2F;**&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; * A class that controls a robotic arm given a learned policy. &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; *&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    public:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    explicit&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;Parameters&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #E36209;&quot;&gt; params&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;    &#x2F;**&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;     * Given the parameters in params_ it extracts the policy info and puts&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;     * them into policyInfo_.&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;     *&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; extractPolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;();&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    private:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      Parameters params_;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      PolicyInfo policyInfo_;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;};&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&#x27;s great that the docstring on &lt;code&gt;extractPolicyInfo()&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; explains what it does. However, comments tend to become outdated and out of sync with the code very quickly. Additionally, if I only look at the code and ignore the comment, it makes me wonder where this data comes from and where it goes. When we find ourselves in this situation we have a couple options:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Guess what the method or function does&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dive one abstraction layer lower and understand the internals&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ask an LLM to dive one abstraction layer lower and summarize the findings.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;None of these options are great. Option 1 leaves us only with a guess. Option 2 forces extra &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;zakirullin&#x2F;cognitive-load?tab=readme-ov-file#cognitive-load-is-what-matters&quot;&gt;cognitive load&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; on every reader, every time. And Option 3 leaves us experiencing &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;syntax-and-servos.ch&#x2F;blog&#x2F;agentic-coding-and-unproductive-success&#x2F;&quot;&gt;unproductive success&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;, getting an answer without building real understanding.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What if we could be more explicit?&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;explicit-code-frees-our-mind-up&quot;&gt;Explicit code frees our mind up&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By adapting the interface to the following we communicate the intent:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;cpp&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;[[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;nodiscard&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;]]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; PolicyInfo extractPolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;const&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; Parameters&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #E36209;&quot;&gt; params&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;&#x2F;**&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; * A class that controls a robotic arm given a learned policy. &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; *&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    public:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    explicit&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;Parameters&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #E36209;&quot;&gt; params&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    private:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      PolicyInfo policyInfo_;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;};&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;&#x2F;&#x2F; Implementation&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;::&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(Parameters params)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    :&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; policyInfo_&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;extractPolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(params)) {}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now we can use &lt;code&gt;extractPolicyInfo(const Parameters&amp;amp; params)&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; in the constructor, using the &lt;code&gt;params&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; parameter. The change is minimal but makes the code explicit and communicates the intent. With a single look at the function declaration we can deduce that it extracts the policy information from the given parameters.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beyond communicating the intent better, there are other positive changes:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reduce State&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: By giving &lt;code&gt;params&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; explicitly to the &lt;code&gt;extractPolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; method in the constructor there is no need to store it in the &lt;code&gt;params_&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; instance variable. Less state is good because it reduces cognitive load. We are also explicit about what we actually use inside &lt;code&gt;LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Improved Testability&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: The free standing function becomes trivial to test as input and output are easily accessible.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Possible Dependency Injection&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: We could refactor &lt;code&gt;LearningMotionController&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; further such that it only takes what it actually needs: a &lt;code&gt;PolicyInfo&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The next time you write a method that has no return values, no arguments or both ask yourself: Is the intent of this method obvious to fellow engineers and your future self? If not, then refactor your code to be explicit about its intent!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.britannica.com&#x2F;dictionary&#x2F;craft&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;craft&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To make or produce something with care or skill.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Software is a craft. Every software craftsman produces code with care and skill, but not everyone who produces code is a craftsman. Let&#x27;s explore what software craftsman have in common besides the fascination for their craft!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;hr &#x2F;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-piece-of-art&quot;&gt;A piece of art&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When usability, aesthetics and functionality are melted into one, we have a piece of art. Behind such a piece is craftsman. Someone who loves what they do and excels at it. The result sparks joy when used while fulfilling its purpose. For industries where the output is a physical product, craftsmanship is often easy to spot. For example, a beautifully designed and crafted wooden peppermill is easily distinguished from a mass market product that only focuses on functionality. In that case we associate aesthetics with a &lt;em&gt;piece of art&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;. But that‘s where the comparison with a piece of (software) art from a craftsman breaks down. What is aesthetic when it comes to software: the source code? The front-end? While the former is only visible by the craftsman, the latter can easily be deceiving. It is much easier to hide sloppy code behind a nice looking front than it is to use bad quality wood to create a good looking wooden cabinet. Especially in the age of LLM where making a good looking &quot;product&quot; is a matter of hours. While it is harder to grasp what the output of a software craftsman looks like we can describe the craftsmen themselves. Independent of AI tooling usage, they all share a rather common set of traits:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;common-traits-of-software-craftsmen&quot;&gt;Common traits of software craftsmen&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A love for their craft&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: The programming languages they use are more than just tools. They are fascinated by their internals, the roadmap of the language designers and the abstract concepts simplifying complex problems. They &quot;inhale&quot; abstract concept for fun and build their own tooling, similiar to how a handyman builds jigs.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A fascination that goes beyond work&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: They work on technical projects outside work. Not to optimize their portfolio for their next job but simply because they enjoy it. They get satisfaction, even relaxation from solving technical challenges or exploring new solutions and technologies.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A pull towards other craftsmen&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: Whenever I found a craftsman in an organization there was almost always more than one. Craftsmen in an organization gravitate towards each other. I observed that if they don&#x27;t find others they usually leave quite quickly, to join other craftsmen somewhere else.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These traits make craftsmen inspiring people. They share their excitement with others, inspiring them. There is another group of people who share these traits: artists. They also love their craft, are fascinated by it and are pulled towards other artists.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When looking at them in a business context, there is a significant difference: craftsmen are aware of business objectives, artists tend to do their work for the sake of the art piece. At the same time, a craftsmen would say that a suboptimal solution achieved with minimal resources that works right before an important customer demo is a piece of art. Are we robotic software engineers therefore all artists?&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: ROS&#x2F;ROS 2 is the best tool to get started with robotics and do prototyping, but some of its strengths turn into challenges when you start developing for a safety critical product that must scale. Fortunately, other open source tools are more suitable for the job. This post is the kick-off on our learning journey, moving to a world beyond ROS .&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-case-for-ros&quot;&gt;The case for ROS&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ROS 2 &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;index.html#&quot;&gt;[1]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;, the Robot Operating System 2 and successor of ROS, is the de facto standard for robotics platforms. Everyone dealing with robotics has heard of it, and most would be lost without it. The whole ecosystem has grown to a one-stop shop for students, educators, researchers and start-ups. It supplies almost everything you need to create the software for your own robot, from sensor drivers to visualization tools, state-of-the-art algorithms and all the glue that keeps it together. Researchers &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;PRBonn&#x2F;kiss-icp&quot;&gt;[2]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; often provide their new algorithms as ROS packages, ready for you to try out. Most even provide data sets recorded as rosbags, i.e. recorded ROS message data, to let others validate or play around with their work. Companies provide ROS drivers &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ouster-lidar&#x2F;ouster-ros&quot;&gt;[3]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; to accelerate the adoption of their hardware within the robotics community. Combine all of this and you get an ecosystem whose value for prototyping is difficult to overstate.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;when-ros-becomes-a-problem&quot;&gt;When ROS Becomes a Problem&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately ROS 2&#x27;s breadth comes at a cost, and it shows as soon as you move beyond prototyping and research. If you want to move towards professional product development, i.e. leaving the lab for testing and deploying robots in the real world, people will start to ask questions. Questions about safety, real time capability, software development processes and worst of all, which part of your software stack is ready to be certified for your target use case. You may be able to ignore this in the beginning, but you will have to answer at some point. It is either your investors, customers or worst case the legal advisors because things went really wrong. The following statements show some, but by far not all issues that can arise when using ROS 2 as the foundation for your product.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DDS as the default middleware&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ROS 2 ships with DDS &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.omg.org&#x2F;omg-dds-portal&#x2F;&quot;&gt;[4]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; (Data Distribution Service) as its default middleware. It is technically no longer mandatory as &lt;code&gt;rmw_zenoh&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_zenoh&quot;&gt;[5]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; and &lt;code&gt;rmw_iceoryx&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_iceoryx&quot;&gt;[6]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; are available as non-DDS RMW alternatives, but DDS remains the default. The DDS API is hidden from the ROS 2 user, reducing parts of its complexity and capabilities. Research shows ROS 2 can introduce up to 50% latency overhead compared to using DDS communications directly, with end-to-end latency strongly dependent on the chosen DDS implementation &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.researchgate.net&#x2F;publication&#x2F;348295821_Latency_Overhead_of_ROS2_for_Modular_Time-Critical_Systems&quot;&gt;[7]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;.
Plenty of DDS related issues were reported over time which led to the conclusion that DDS might not be the best tool for robotics if the bulk of your work happens on one host. Read this post &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;discourse.openrobotics.org&#x2F;t&#x2F;ros-2-alternative-middleware-report&#x2F;33771&quot;&gt;[8]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; and the attached report to learn more about the technical issues and the complexity, which led to Zenoh being supported as a Tier-1 ROS 2 middleware. While some DDS vendors offer shared memory as an option for intra-host communication, you still pay the overhead of DDS discovery, Quality-of-Service management and the like. DDS has its place in robotics, but it takes a conscious decision which middleware suits your application best.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A dedicated build system&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If we ignore the ROS 2 middleware issues, there are plenty of other annoyances left.
The &lt;code&gt;colcon&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;ament_cmake&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; stack simplifies many common project setups but adds a layer of abstraction that makes tailored setups difficult and sometimes impossible to achieve. Every ROS 2 package requires both a &lt;code&gt;package.xml&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; and a &lt;code&gt;CMakeLists.txt&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;, and the &lt;code&gt;package.xml&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; must declare all dependencies for &lt;code&gt;colcon&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; to resolve the build order &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;Tutorials&#x2F;Beginner-Client-Libraries&#x2F;Creating-Your-First-ROS2-Package.html&quot;&gt;[9]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;. While these abstractions help getting you started, they limit flexibility in adopting refined workflows and modern tools by locking you into an ecosystem that is hard to escape. In a perfect world, your middleware should not limit your higher level design decisions and tool setup.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A deep and opaque dependency footprint&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every C++ ROS 2 node, as minimalistic as it may be, links against &lt;code&gt;rclcpp&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;, which has a ton of transitive dependencies &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;blob&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;CMakeLists.txt&quot;&gt;[10]&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;. This shows the cost of convenience. ROS 2 handles lots of use cases and provides alternative middleware, all of this is abstracted away from the user and handled under the hood. Again, this was designed on purpose for its specific use case, but may not be appropriate for a slim and efficient production system.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No schema evolution&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If a .msg file is changed, meaning a field is added, removed, renamed or reordered, every ROS 2 node in the system using this message must be recompiled against the new definition. There is no mechanism for forwards or backwards compatibility. A subscriber compiled against v1 of a message can&#x27;t receive messages from a publisher compiled against v2.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While this may sound like a trivial problem, it is quite the opposite. As a system evolves, the interfaces usually evolve with it. If every single change in  your ROS 2 messages is a breaking change, you have to be very good at versioning to keep your collected rosbags in line with your system. In the age of AI, data is money. Losing data is a critical issue. Every message change is a fork in your dataset history, and you have to handle the incompatibilities in your tools.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imagine you collected data from the field for several months, spent hours on data curation and tens of thousands of dollars on the operation. Now you finally decide to fix a typo in your &lt;code&gt;Odometry.msg&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; and ... your ROS stack can&#x27;t read the data anymore. In practice, interface changes are inevitable, but the underlying problem will remain. You will have to spend the effort to handle the changes, as minor as they may be. Fortunately, other IDLs like FlatBuffers handle this case more gracefully, where fields can be added or deprecated without breaking existing data.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The hardware reality&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most robotics products don&#x27;t run on your trusty old desktop PC, but on embedded compute platforms, specifically ARM-based SoCs with constrained memory and limited compute. Cross-compilation from x86 machines is part of everyday life, and subtle architectural differences like memory ordering behavior can turn your code from &quot;working in simulation&quot; into a nightmare of silent bugs on the target platform.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On these systems, the dominant communication pattern is intra-host. Dozens of nodes exchange all the data they need, from small control messages at hundreds of Hz to large lidar pointclouds at a few frames per second. This is the environment where all of the problems listed above add up. The resource-devouring ecosystem, the endless transitive dependencies and the custom build tools that were not made for this application, they all pile up into a significant mess. You can spend your investor&#x27;s money on solving this problem to create the necessary foundation of your product, or you could try to choose appropriate tools for the job and invest in what actually defines your product.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The list goes on&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I don&#x27;t want to go into more details here, the web is full of evidence what ROS 2 can and can&#x27;t do, but if you are just starting on your product development journey you should spend some time on this decision. Review how successful robotics companies run their software and how much they rely on ROS 2. What you will find is that many established robotics scale-ups moved away from ROS 1 or 2 at this point. As I mentioned before, there is a good reason why people start with ROS 2, but there are plenty of reasons to move away from it as soon as you leave the experimentation phase.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;how-to-move-on&quot;&gt;How to move on&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After reading about all those problems you probably want solutions. A link to a package or repo to download, maybe a 10-sentence guide how to replace ROS and finally make big bucks in robotics. Unfortunately there is no quick win like this, but the latest developments make it easier than ever to transition to a new middleware without losing all the nice utilities that ROS provided for introspection and other important tasks. The posts in our &quot;Beyond ROS&quot; series will take you along to learn how to use:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Zero-Copy IPC with Iceoryx2&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: A zero-copy middleware for intra-host communication. Replacing ROS&#x2F;DDS for high performance and minimal latency communication.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;FlatBuffers as a Universal Serialization Layer&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: A cross-platform serialization library for performance critical applications enabling forwards and backwards compatibility.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Building ROS-like Ergonomics&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: Replacing publish&#x2F;subscribe patterns, services, actions and the parameter server with the new middleware.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Architectural Choices for Stable Products&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: How to leverage your new middleware&#x27;s features for maximized performance.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rethinking TF2&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: Bringing the transformation library to the new middleware.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Debugging and Observability&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: How to replace the beloved &lt;code&gt;ros2 topic echo&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; and RViz in our new system.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you have to give up&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: Nothing is for free. Learn about the downsides of this transition to be able to make well informed decisions.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While we cover many theoretical concepts and learn about what makes a great middleware and which tools you really need, we will also keep it practical and provide working code examples in a playground repository, including a docker image with all required dependencies and some Rust sprinkled in for good measure.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Stay tuned for the next posts in this series, where we cover the basics of Iceoryx2 and Flatbuffers by writing and running some code. Join us for the journey beyond rapid prototyping and research in robotics, towards a production ready robotic system.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;resources&quot;&gt;Resources&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;ROS 2 Documentation (Rolling)&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, 2026 — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;index.html&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;index.html&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vizzo, I., Guadagnino, T., Mersch, B., Wiesmann, L., Behley, J., Stachniss, C. — &lt;em&gt;KISS-ICP: In Defense of Point-to-Point ICP — Simple, Accurate, and Robust Registration If Done the Right Way&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters, 2023 — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;PRBonn&#x2F;kiss-icp&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;PRBonn&#x2F;kiss-icp&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ouster — &lt;em&gt;ouster-ros: Official ROS driver for Ouster sensors&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, 2024 — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ouster-lidar&#x2F;ouster-ros&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ouster-lidar&#x2F;ouster-ros&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Object Management Group — &lt;em&gt;DDS (Data Distribution Service) Portal&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.omg.org&#x2F;omg-dds-portal&#x2F;&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.omg.org&#x2F;omg-dds-portal&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;rmw_zenoh: ROS 2 RMW implementation using Zenoh&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_zenoh&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_zenoh&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;rmw_iceoryx2: ROS 2 RMW implementation using iceoryx&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_iceoryx&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rmw_iceoryx&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kronauer, T., Pohlmann, J., Matthé, M., Smejkal, T., Fettweis, G. — &lt;em&gt;Latency Overhead of ROS2 for Modular Time-Critical Systems&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, arXiv:2101.02074, 2021 — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;arxiv.org&#x2F;abs&#x2F;2101.02074&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;arxiv.org&#x2F;abs&#x2F;2101.02074&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;ROS 2 Alternative Middleware Report&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, ROS Discourse, 2023 — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;discourse.openrobotics.org&#x2F;t&#x2F;ros-2-alternative-middleware-report&#x2F;33771&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;discourse.openrobotics.org&#x2F;t&#x2F;ros-2-alternative-middleware-report&#x2F;33771&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;Creating Your First ROS 2 Package&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, ROS 2 Documentation (Rolling) — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;Tutorials&#x2F;Beginner-Client-Libraries&#x2F;Creating-Your-First-ROS2-Package.html&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.ros.org&#x2F;en&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;Tutorials&#x2F;Beginner-Client-Libraries&#x2F;Creating-Your-First-ROS2-Package.html&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open Robotics — &lt;em&gt;rclcpp&#x2F;CMakeLists.txt (rolling branch)&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; — &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;blob&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;CMakeLists.txt&quot;&gt;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;ros2&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;blob&#x2F;rolling&#x2F;rclcpp&#x2F;CMakeLists.txt&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: We engineers have a very limited context window and robots have a lot of state to be kept track of in software and our heads. Let&#x27;s make our lives easier by making invalid state unrepresentable! Then the compiler or type checker becomes your best friend and frees up essential space in your head by taking care of all these errors at compile time!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;parse-don-t-validate&quot;&gt;Parse, don&#x27;t validate&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I first encountered the concept of &quot;parse, don&#x27;t validate&quot; in &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lexi-lambda.github.io&#x2F;blog&#x2F;2019&#x2F;11&#x2F;05&#x2F;parse-don-t-validate&#x2F;&quot;&gt;Alexis King&#x27;s article&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;. Even though the examples are in haskell, a language I&#x27;m not familiar with, they resonated immediately with me. The &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.harudagondi.space&#x2F;blog&#x2F;parse-dont-validate-and-type-driven-design-in-rust&#x2F;&quot;&gt;recent article of the same pattern in Rust by Gio&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; reminded me once again why it is so powerful. The idea is simple: Incorporate as much information as possible in the types such that the compiler or type checker can validate it at compile time or type check time respectively.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&#x27;s dive into it with &lt;strong&gt;real world robotics&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; examples in python and C++ from the &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;gravisrobotics.com&quot;&gt;Gravis Robotics&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; codebase!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;python-example-leveraging-wrapper-types-to-avoid-wrong-robot-identifier&quot;&gt;Python Example: Leveraging wrapper types to avoid wrong robot identifier&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note: Python supports typing out of the box, however needs third party tooling for type verification such as  &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;pyrefly.org&quot;&gt;pyrefly&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;, &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;DetachHead&#x2F;basedpyright&quot;&gt;basedpyright&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; or &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.astral.sh&#x2F;ty&#x2F;&quot;&gt;ty&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;. We recommend to leverage these tools similar to a compiler, to verify types at type check time (e.g. in editor, CI or both)&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;gravisrobotics.com&quot;&gt;Gravis&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; each robot has a name. e.g. &lt;code&gt;bob-the-builder&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. In addition each machine has a globally unique identifier: &lt;code&gt;robot&#x2F;bob-the-builder&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; (we use the &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.cloud.google.com&#x2F;apis&#x2F;design&quot;&gt;Google API guideline&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; as a reference). For historical reasons we often use the robot name in on-robot software without the &lt;code&gt;robot&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; prefix. As you imagine we eventually had bugs in our software where the robot name was used instead of the identifier and vice versa. They are not hard to track down, but annoying. The newtype pattern in combination with a type checker eliminates the possibility of this class of problems.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Error prone code would look like this:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;python&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; TaskDefinition&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    def&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; __init__&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(self, robot:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; str&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, ...):&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;        # The globally unique identifier of the robot&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;        self&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;._robot:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; str&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; robot&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;        ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; TaskExecutor&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    def&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; execute&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(self, task_definition: TaskDefinition):&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;        ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;def&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; start_task&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(task_executor: TaskExecutor): &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    robot_name:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; str&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #032F62;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;robo42&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; # Note: no &amp;quot;robot&#x2F;&amp;quot; prefix&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    task_definition&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; TaskDefinition(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #E36209;&quot;&gt;robot&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;robot_name,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    task_executor.execute(task_definition) &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note how we have semantic information in the comment in &lt;code&gt;TaskDefinition&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. We &lt;strong&gt;hope&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; that fellow engineers read the comments and adhere to it. In the best case the &lt;code&gt;task_executor&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; does validation on the &lt;code&gt;task_definition&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; and fails quickly. In the worst case it will fail only sometime later, when a piece of code assumes that &lt;code&gt;task_definition._robot&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; is prefixed with &lt;code&gt;robot&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. This increases our &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;minds.md&#x2F;zakirullin&#x2F;cognitive&quot;&gt;cognitive load&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;, which takes energy from the actual task we are working on.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&#x27;s fix this by incorporating that information into the type system such that the type checker can help us.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By introducing a &lt;code&gt;RobotIdentifier&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; type we can distinguish the robot name from the identifier and design an API that makes it impossible to mix up the two things:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;python&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;from&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; typing&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; import&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; NewType&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;RobotName&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; NewType(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #032F62;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;RobotName&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; str&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;RobotIdentifier&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; NewType(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #032F62;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;RobotIdentifier&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; str&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;def&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; robot_identifier_from_name&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(name: RobotName) -&amp;gt; RobotIdentifier:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; RobotIdentifier(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;f&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #032F62;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;robot&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;{&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;name&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #032F62;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now we change the &lt;code&gt;TaskDefinition&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; to accept a &lt;code&gt;RobotIdentifier&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; instead of a &lt;code&gt;str&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;python&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;class&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; TaskDefinition&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;    def&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt; __init__&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(self, robot: RobotIdentifier, ...):&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;        # The globally unique identifier of the robot&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;        self&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;._robot: RobotIdentifier&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; robot&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;        ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this small change, we have made the code safe for accidential mixups of robot names and global robot identifiers. The typechecker will report&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Argument `str` is not assignable to parameter  `robot` with type `RobotIdentifier` in function `TaskDefinition.__init__`&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Not only are we now very explicit in the API that we expect a global robot identifier we also provide the semantic information to the type checker, which can warn us if we use the wrong type when creating the &lt;code&gt;TaskDefinition&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. Just by introducing these new types we eliminated a whole category of errors!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;c-example-migrating-hardware-version-numbers&quot;&gt;C++ Example: Migrating hardware version numbers&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We recently changed our hardware versioning schema and needed to propagate this change to our code. The schema changed from &lt;code&gt;123&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; (a plain number, but often used with a prefix, e.g. &lt;code&gt;hw-123&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;) to &lt;code&gt;hw-9876-123456&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. The old serial number was accessed via a free standing function:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;cpp&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;import &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;cstdint&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt;&#x2F;**&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; * Returns the serial number of the hardware.&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6A737D;&quot;&gt; *&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;[[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;nodiscard&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;]]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; uint32_t&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; getSerialNumber&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;();&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As you can see the old format was just represented as an &lt;code&gt;uint32_t&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. Remember that we have hardware of different versions concurrently in the field. Therefore we expect hardware with the old and the new format to live concurrently for quite a while. The quick change here would have been to just change the return type of &lt;code&gt;getSerialNumber()&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;std::string&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; and use the function as is. I.e. &lt;code&gt;getSerialNumber()&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; would return a string that either looks like &lt;code&gt;123&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;hw-9876-123456&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. The type system would help us find all the usages as existing callers expect an &lt;code&gt;uint32_t&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; instead of a string. Let&#x27;s improve this further by giving even more information to the compiler to support us further! We give the compiler information about both types of serial numbers, old and new ones:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #24292E; background-color: #FFFFFF;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;cpp&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;import &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;cstdint&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;import &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;string&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;import &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;variant&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;using&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; SerialNumber&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; std&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;::&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;variant&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D73A49;&quot;&gt;uint32_t&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; std&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;::&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;string&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;[[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt;nodiscard&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #005CC5;&quot;&gt;]]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #6F42C1;&quot;&gt; SerialNumber getSerialNumber&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(); &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this, we force our future selves to think about both cases whenever we use the &lt;code&gt;getSerialNumber()&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; function. This prevents a software engineer not familiar with new serial numbers just prepending &lt;code&gt;hw-&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; to whatever they get back from &lt;code&gt;getSerialNumber()&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. Note that exactly this situation would create a bug that we would only detect at runtime. By forcing the caller to take care of both variants we move these mistakes to compile time!&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note: It might be tempting to use &lt;code&gt;using LegacySerialNumer = uint32_t&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; to create something similar to python&#x27;s &lt;code&gt;NewType&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;. This does not work as &lt;code&gt;using&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; only creates a type alias. There are other options however. For example Boost&#x27;s  &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.boost.org&#x2F;doc&#x2F;libs&#x2F;1_61_0&#x2F;libs&#x2F;serialization&#x2F;doc&#x2F;strong_typedef.html&quot;&gt;BOOST_STRONG_TYPEDEF&lt;&#x2F;a&gt; library or defining custom structs or classes as described in this &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.fluentcpp.com&#x2F;2016&#x2F;12&#x2F;05&#x2F;named-constructors&#x2F;&quot;&gt;fluentcpp article&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;. And there is first proof of concepts of doing this with &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.reddit.com&#x2F;r&#x2F;cpp&#x2F;comments&#x2F;1rgo4s9&#x2F;power_of_c26_reflection_strong_opaque_type&#x2F;&quot;&gt;reflection in C++26&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;summary&quot;&gt;Summary&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If illegal state is unrepresentable, the compiler will help you avoid it. In compiled languages, it will be compile time errors and in dynamic languages, a type checker can help us avoid it. To get there you need to give the information to the compiler&#x2F;type checker to be able to help you. Therefore, don&#x27;t be mean and withhold information from the compiler.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Make the compiler a better friend by giving it what it needs to help you!&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding software used to be a byproduct of building it. Getting a feature to work meant grappling with the code: wrong abstractions, incomplete mental models, problems that turned out to be different from what you thought. The efficiency agentic coding offers comes from removing this struggle, and with it, the conditions from which deep understanding can be earned.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consider two ways of understanding a codebase. The reviewer&#x27;s understanding comes from observation. They read a design document and a pull request, verify that the proposed approach fits the system&#x27;s architecture and handles its edge cases. The author&#x27;s understanding comes from creation. They try approaches that don&#x27;t work, discover constraints they didn&#x27;t anticipate, and reshape their solution as their understanding of the system deepens. Both understand why the chosen solution works, but only the author understands why they discarded the alternatives.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You have to be frustrated injecting a dependency through three layers of abstraction to finally realize your abstractions are wrong. You have to spend a puzzled afternoon chasing a segfault before you discover it&#x27;s due to newly shared state between two subsystems you rarely touch. Author-level understanding includes &lt;em&gt;negative knowledge&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;. It&#x27;s the map of the solution space, not just one solution that works. No amount of careful reviewing produces it.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Learning theory (the human kind) has a name for this struggle. The unsuccessful attempt that precedes a working solution is called a &lt;em&gt;productive failure&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;, and there&#x27;s robust evidence that it produces deeper understanding than being taught the correct solution directly [1-4]. The learning-theory term that applies to agentic coding is &lt;em&gt;unproductive success&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An unproductive success is when a task completes but the understanding it produces is shallow. When I delegate work to Claude and it offers a correct solution, I&#x27;m left with reviewer-level understanding. It creates, I observe. What&#x27;s treacherous is that this unproductive success is self-concealing. There&#x27;s no immediate signal that anything was lost.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even if coding agents become good enough to complete any well-specified task, someone still has to specify the task. Understanding what to build and diagnosing why what you built doesn&#x27;t work both require the negative knowledge that only authorship yields. I let agents write code for me all the time. But the algorithm that touches core business logic? The API that every future module depends on? That I write myself.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Understanding software was always an investment. Before agentic coding, you didn&#x27;t have the choice not to invest if you wanted something built. Now you do.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&quot;references&quot;&gt;References&lt;&#x2F;h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[1] &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;doi.org&#x2F;10.1080&#x2F;00461520.2016.1155457&quot;&gt;Kapur, M. (2016). Examining Productive Failure, Productive Success, Unproductive Failure, and Unproductive Success in Learning. Educational Psychologist, 51(2), 289-299.&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[2] &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;doi.org&#x2F;10.1111&#x2F;cogs.12107&quot;&gt;Kapur, M. (2014). Productive Failure in Learning Math. Cognitive Science, 38(5), 1008-1022.&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[3] &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;doi.org&#x2F;10.7551&#x2F;mitpress%2F4561.003.0011&quot;&gt;Bjork, R.A. (1994). Memory and metamemory considerations in the training of human beings. In J. Metcalfe and A. Shimamura (Eds.), Metacognition: Knowing about knowing (pp. 185-205). Cambridge, MA: MIT Press.&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[4] &lt;a rel=&quot;external&quot; href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;arxiv.org&#x2F;pdf&#x2F;2601.20245&quot;&gt;Judy Hanwen Shen, Alex Tamkin (2026). How AI Impacts Skill Formation&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
